{"kind":"assert","text":"Hugh: duplicate cleanup needs one named survivor and one coordinator.\n\nThe history audit found a concrete way unfinished work disappears even when both authors intend to preserve it. Codex withdrew #6466 because Claude's #6467 supposedly survived. Claude withdrew #6467 because Codex's #6466 supposedly survived. Both retirements are effective. A review then cited #6466 as the governing follow-up, but neither assignment survived and the [mutation matrix](notes/2026-08-08-kernel-mutation-matrix.md@414533873740ca330c424a037b3e0ed796bfc1cd:11) still carries the obsolete recipes. The [paired history and current mutation evidence](crossed-duplicate-evidence.json) records the exact requests, conditions and retirements. Recovery request #20743 / 1d10de789579640d37cd0bcafc7292eb7bea4a30 now assigns one complete repair to Claude.\n\nRecommendation: when duplicate assignments cross, choose one coordinator and one explicit canonical request before either author retires anything. Check that the canonical request preserves all conditions and remains effective, and that the performer has accepted it or states why it is queued. Each retiring author names that same survivor; the coordinator then checks that the surviving request and its remaining outcome are still present. If they are not, restore the assignment immediately. A citation to another request is useful evidence, but is not itself a guard guaranteeing that request will survive.\n\nThis is a small addition to existing intake and retirement discipline, using existing requests, promises and supersession. The [guarded reassignment helper](SKILL.md@414533873740ca330c424a037b3e0ed796bfc1cd:231) handles moving one unclaimed request; it does not resolve two independently filed duplicates. Ordinary [request withdrawal](docs/reference/gs/supersede.md@414533873740ca330c424a037b3e0ed796bfc1cd:62) releases a promise, so even seeing a claim is not a substitute for coordinating which request will be kept.\n\nRetain this case for the existing retirement-safeguard design #20013 and the operational examples in #20305. Any proposed automatic safeguard should be checked against this two-request case as well as single-request replacement. This note proposes no new lifecycle state and authorizes no additional implementation. It sharpens the earlier no-silent-drop recommendation with a reproduced cause and a minimal coordination step.","body":{"head":"414533873740ca330c424a037b3e0ed796bfc1cd","stale_bases":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:a18edda93c0c33185ae944093fbc7b997b583568 stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:001b2e3b06105d22946682cb90201bc66c1c6a26, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0a6f748b48ce807b2efbe9f4c8c245dda4d90359, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:142e574a4fd1e3ebfd9ff4d63259385a7aa78424, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:1d08a228cd15b7bfda5ce91a859f0aea79b94193 and 56 more"}}